# {{.Name}} — frontend

A React application built by [Vite](https://vite.dev). It is the whole of this
project's user interface; the Go side is a JSON API.

## You do not run this directly

```bash
ry dev     # starts the Go server and Vite together
ry build   # compiles this into dist/, then embeds dist/ in the binary
```

`ry dev` starts Vite on a port of its own and the Go server proxies everything
it did not route to it, so there is one address to open — the one `ry dev`
prints. Editing a file under `src/` updates the running page without a reload;
editing a `.go` file rebuilds the server and reloads the browser.

The scripts in `package.json` still work if you want Vite on its own
(`npm run dev`), but calls to `/api` will 404: nothing is behind it then.

## How it reaches the API

Same origin, absolute paths, no base URL:

```js
import { get, post } from "./api.js";

const service = await get("/api");
```

`/api` is exempt from CSRF checks, which is why `api.js` sends no token. Add
your endpoints under that prefix in `routes/web.go` and they inherit the same
treatment.

## The build is embedded

`vite build` writes `dist/`, and `resources/embed.go` compiles that directory
into the Go binary. Deployment stays one file with nothing beside it — which is
the reason this framework is written in Go, and the reason `dist/` must keep
existing even when empty. The `.gitkeep` inside it is load-bearing: an embed
directive that matches nothing will not compile, and without it a clone with no
Node installed could not build the Go code at all.

## Dependencies

```bash
npm install          # `ry dev` and `ry build` do this for you on the first run
npm outdated         # what has moved on
```
